{"commit": "2a62f8c3cd643ccabea9034b71f9c9e97529f4e3", "id": "2a62f8c3cd643ccabea9034b71f9c9e97529f4e3-1", "language": "rust", "reason": "Proposal names no upstream test", "repo": "https://github.com/jamesmunns/postcard", "reproduction": {"attempt": "20260924T002228Z-0bf1be50", "evidence_sha256": {"evidence/2a62f8c3cd643ccabea9034b71f9c9e97529f4e3-1/20260924T002228Z-0bf1be50/origin.json": "3170b68d7e3a9602983943ef02fda1de85904730fcff391433b20bb778a15fdd", "evidence/2a62f8c3cd643ccabea9034b71f9c9e97529f4e3-1/20260924T002228Z-0bf1be50/proposal.json": "d081d09197313054ffc5e840a75391d022020412702ad97f4628da47b3f3fe50"}, "provenance": "verbatim"}, "shard": "postcard", "status": "blocked", "subject": "Fix tuple struct deserialization bug", "summary": "`Deserializer::deserialize_tuple_struct` discarded its `len` argument and forwarded to `deserialize_seq`. The serializer for a tuple struct calls `serialize_tuple` (no varint length prefix), but `deserialize_seq` expects a leading varint length, so the decoder's length cursor consumed a payload byte and every subsequent field was misaligned. The fix forwards to `deserialize_tuple(len, visitor)` so the wire shapes line up.", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T00:22:28.745552+00:00"}
{"commit": "77fd54b18977e8491ebe1b67cc0d104e32ab02d6", "id": "77fd54b18977e8491ebe1b67cc0d104e32ab02d6-1", "language": "rust", "reason": "Proposal names no upstream test", "repo": "https://github.com/jamesmunns/postcard", "reproduction": {"attempt": "20260924T002239Z-fb208440", "evidence_sha256": {"evidence/77fd54b18977e8491ebe1b67cc0d104e32ab02d6-1/20260924T002239Z-fb208440/origin.json": "d522f37e771f3de7ae4fa4e83eb017fce280ea2f86cd6cf8f08df05f73fbfa27", "evidence/77fd54b18977e8491ebe1b67cc0d104e32ab02d6-1/20260924T002239Z-fb208440/proposal.json": "ea9c775d2dae7068508759deede203b9848fa2072011b929b391051ec603b282"}, "provenance": "verbatim"}, "shard": "postcard", "status": "blocked", "subject": "Potential fix for collect_str bug", "summary": "`Serializer::collect_str` was `unreachable!()`, so any type whose `Serialize` impl funnels through it (the canonical case is `chrono`'s timestamp types) panicked at runtime. The fix runs the formatter twice: once into a counting writer to learn the byte length, then once into the real flavor after writing a varint length prefix \u2014 matching the wire format of a regular `serialize_str`.", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T00:22:39.328195+00:00"}
